sports cars on the road today.

32
00:02:37,961 --> 00:02:42,620
The difference is what goes into the engine.

33
00:02:42,621 --> 00:02:45,120
The secret to making between 7,000 and 8,000 horsepower

34
00:02:45,121 --> 00:02:47,420
on one of these eight-cylinder engines is the fuel.

35
00:02:47,421 --> 00:02:49,560
We pour nitromethane into the fuel tank.

36
00:02:49,561 --> 00:02:52,700
We distribute it with a massive fuel pump into the engine.

37
00:02:52,701 --> 00:02:54,600
We put the air into the engine through an injector,

38
00:02:54,601 --> 00:02:56,400
which has got a supercharger under it.

39
00:02:56,401 --> 00:02:57,860
It sucks all the air into the engine.

40
00:02:58,200 --> 00:02:59,880
We mix those two in each cylinder.

41
00:03:00,240 --> 00:03:03,180
We light it with a big magneto, and that's what makes all the power.

42
00:03:09,340 --> 00:03:12,140
Nitromethane is like gasoline on steroids.

43
00:03:13,660 --> 00:03:17,940
To give it an explosive boost, the fuel supplies its own oxygen atoms

44
00:03:17,941 --> 00:03:20,160
to make it burn at an accelerated rate.

45
00:03:22,980 --> 00:03:26,140
15 pounds of air is needed to burn one pound of gasoline,

46
00:03:26,400 --> 00:03:31,400
Nitro needs less than two pounds of air to burn one pound of fuel.

47
00:03:31,401 --> 00:03:36,400
This means eight times more nitro methane can be pumped into a cylinder.

48
00:03:36,401 --> 00:03:40,400
The more fuel burned, the higher the horsepower.

49
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:50,400
In one quarter mile, five second run, a dragster will burn up to 15 gallons of nitro.

50
00:03:50,401 --> 00:03:53,400
That's a sixtieth of a mile per gallon.

51
00:03:53,401 --> 00:03:58,400
If you're looking for fuel economy, this isn't it.

52
00:03:58,401 --> 00:04:01,400
Nitromethane over the years has proven

53
00:04:01,401 --> 00:04:03,400
to be a very powerful fuel.

54
00:04:03,401 --> 00:04:05,400
We've learned to tame it and put it into these engines

55
00:04:05,401 --> 00:04:07,400
to make the maximum amount of horsepower

56
00:04:07,401 --> 00:04:09,400
with the air ratio that we have.

57
00:04:09,401 --> 00:04:12,400
But nitromethane is also extremely volatile,

58
00:04:14,400 --> 00:04:17,400
as Melanie Troxell discovered when her engine

59
00:04:17,401 --> 00:04:18,400
exploded during a race.

60
00:04:18,401 --> 00:04:19,400
during a race.

61
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:25,200
Luckily, she escaped without injury and even won the race.

62
00:04:27,400 --> 00:04:30,520
In their heyday, drag racers like Melanie and Tommy Johnson,

63
00:04:30,521 --> 00:04:34,080
Jr. backed up their bravado by harnessing as much horsepower

64
00:04:34,081 --> 00:04:34,781
as possible.

65
00:04:37,580 --> 00:04:40,320
But oddly, like a lot of us, they have trouble defining what

66
00:04:40,321 --> 00:04:41,720
horsepower actually is.

67
00:04:45,600 --> 00:04:47,600
Horsepower is a tough question to answer.

68
00:04:47,601 --> 00:04:50,260
Well, it's the power of one horse, right?

69
00:04:52,960 --> 00:04:54,340
Most people don't realize it,

70
00:04:54,380 --> 00:04:56,340
but there is an exact definition of a horsepower,

71
00:04:56,580 --> 00:04:59,100
which is 33,000 foot-pounds per minute.

72
00:04:59,840 --> 00:05:02,380
If you didn't quite get that, don't worry.

73
00:05:02,920 --> 00:05:05,700
Just remember that horsepower is work done over time.

74
00:05:07,340 --> 00:05:09,420
I've got about 100 of you under my hood, yeah.

75
00:05:10,640 --> 00:05:12,820
But you may wonder where the horse comes in.

76
00:05:12,821 --> 00:05:21,820
In the late 1700s, James Watt was the struggling inventor of a new type of steam engine, and

77
00:05:21,821 --> 00:05:23,820
also a savvy salesman.

78
00:05:23,821 --> 00:05:28,780
And since his customers were basically using horses to do their work, he needed to be able

79
00:05:28,781 --> 00:05:32,340
to tell his customers, look, I have a steam engine here that will replace two of your

80
00:05:32,341 --> 00:05:34,420
horses or four of your horses.

81
00:05:34,421 --> 00:05:38,340
And in order to do that, he had to actually go out and watch a horse.

82
00:05:38,341 --> 00:05:44,020
There are a few theories about how James Watt created the concept of horsepower.

83
00:05:44,021 --> 00:05:49,760
According to the most popular one, he observed a horse on a grinding mill wheel.

84
00:05:49,761 --> 00:05:54,220
It was something like a horse on a device we know today, a hot walker, used to cool

85
00:05:54,221 --> 00:05:57,100
down horses after a workout.

86
00:05:57,101 --> 00:06:04,340
However, the horse Watt saw met resistance when connected to the grinding wheel.

87
00:06:04,341 --> 00:06:08,940
calculated that the horse pulled with a force of 180 pounds.

88
00:06:08,941 --> 00:06:11,240
He multiplied that by the horse's speed, which he

89
00:06:11,241 --> 00:06:15,760
determined to be 181 feet per minute.

90
00:06:15,761 --> 00:06:19,720
After rounding up the number, Watt arrived at nearly 33,000

91
00:06:19,721 --> 00:06:24,460
pounds, lifted one foot per minute.

92
00:06:24,461 --> 00:06:30,200
And voila, he defined horsepower as work done over time.

93
00:06:30,201 --> 00:06:32,600
How does this translate into horsepower for a car engine?

94
00:06:34,340 --> 00:06:37,640
Here's how it works.

95
00:06:37,641 --> 00:06:43,580
An internal combustion engine ignites a combination of fuel and air.

96
00:06:43,581 --> 00:06:46,280
The explosion drives a piston downward.

97
00:06:46,281 --> 00:06:50,560
The piston's movement turns a crankshaft.

98
00:06:50,561 --> 00:06:55,920
The amount of turning force on the crankshaft is known as torque.

99
00:06:55,921 --> 00:07:00,400
An example of torque is when a wrench is used to loosen a nut.

100
00:07:00,401 --> 00:07:03,040
The more force or torque applied to the nut,

101
00:07:03,041 --> 00:07:04,540
the easier it is to unfasten.

102
00:07:08,100 --> 00:07:10,700
So horsepower is the turning force of torque

103
00:07:10,701 --> 00:07:14,420
measured in foot pounds, combined with a number of crankshaft

104
00:07:14,421 --> 00:07:16,680
rotations per minute.

105
00:07:16,681 --> 00:07:19,760
This is work done over time.

106
00:07:19,761 --> 00:07:22,760
And again, one engine generated horsepower

107
00:07:22,761 --> 00:07:27,520
equals 33,000 foot pounds per minute.

108
00:07:27,521 --> 00:07:29,820
How do humans stack up in horsepower generation?

109
00:07:33,040 --> 00:07:38,880
The average person can sustain only about one-tenth horsepower when riding a bicycle.

110
00:07:40,300 --> 00:07:42,740
Even world-class cyclists have their limit.

111
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:48,060
We get tired, we go slower, and eventually come to a stop.

112
00:07:50,240 --> 00:07:51,600
We just don't have the stamina.

113
00:07:51,820 --> 00:07:53,780
We don't have the endurance of a mechanical machine.

114
00:07:54,700 --> 00:07:58,300
Mechanical horsepower became a key selling point for many car makers.

115
00:07:58,760 --> 00:08:02,240
From the early 1900s with a Ford Model T at 10 horsepower,

116
00:08:02,241 --> 00:08:08,900
to the muscle cars of the 50s,

117
00:08:09,340 --> 00:08:12,100
like the 283-horsepower Corvette,

118
00:08:13,100 --> 00:08:15,120
to today's Bugatti Chiron

119
00:08:15,121 --> 00:08:18,800
at a monstrous 1,578 horsepower.

120
00:08:19,800 --> 00:08:22,020
The car industry has sold customers

121
00:08:22,021 --> 00:08:23,860
on ever-increasing amounts of horsepower.

122
00:08:27,140 --> 00:08:29,400
And yet, there was a problem.

123
00:08:30,140 --> 00:08:30,920
From the beginning,

124
00:08:30,921 --> 00:08:34,920
manufacturers measured their own horsepower.

125
00:08:34,921 --> 00:08:38,920
So who was keeping the auto industry honest?

126
00:08:38,921 --> 00:08:45,920
In 1917, an organization called the Society of Automotive Engineers, or SAE,

127
00:08:45,921 --> 00:08:49,920
provided a set of horsepower testing standards.

128
00:08:49,921 --> 00:08:53,920
Back in the early days, they used a very different process,

129
00:08:53,921 --> 00:08:56,920
from company to company, from tester to tester.

130
00:08:56,921 --> 00:09:03,060
What SAE standards tried to do was to establish one formal way of running the test.

131
00:09:04,740 --> 00:09:12,440
From its inception until today, the SAE has tried diligently to make horsepower a measurement car buyers can believe in.

132
00:09:13,760 --> 00:09:19,180
We look at horsepower. If a number is stated as SAE certified power,

133
00:09:19,181 --> 00:09:26,360
that customer has a level of assurance that that's the real number as tested and as tested properly.

134
00:09:26,920 --> 00:09:31,920
Here at a General Motors facility outside Detroit, Michigan,

135
00:09:31,921 --> 00:09:36,920
technicians are testing an engine to ensure it performs up to the 310 horsepower

136
00:09:36,921 --> 00:09:39,920
they'll advertise to the public.

137
00:09:39,921 --> 00:09:42,920
Millions of dollars are riding on the results.

138
00:09:42,921 --> 00:09:44,920
It's a little nerve-wracking.

139
00:09:44,921 --> 00:09:48,920
There's a lot of responsibility involved, and there's a lot of things at stake,

140
00:09:48,921 --> 00:09:52,920
so I hope everything runs smoothly, and I look forward to running it.

141
00:09:52,921 --> 00:09:57,140
An instrument known as a dynamometer is used to measure the horsepower of the engine.

142
00:09:59,100 --> 00:10:01,500
The engine is mounted to the test stand.

143
00:10:01,660 --> 00:10:05,720
It is then connected through a drive shaft to our engine dynamometer.

144
00:10:06,060 --> 00:10:10,580
The dynamometer is what actually measures the output of the engine, measures in horsepower.

145
00:10:11,140 --> 00:10:16,140
The parameters of the dynamometer are then measured and recorded in the engine control room.

146
00:10:16,860 --> 00:10:22,080
The dynamometer measures horsepower by recording the torque and rotations of the crankshaft.

147
00:10:22,920 --> 00:10:28,700
The crankshaft is attached to a rotor inside the dynamometer.

148
00:10:28,701 --> 00:10:33,740
As the rotor spins, an electromagnetic field makes the outside hull of the dynamometer

149
00:10:33,741 --> 00:10:37,380
want to turn in the opposite direction.

150
00:10:37,381 --> 00:10:42,420
To measure torque, an arm exerts resistance force on the dynamometer hull, keeping it

151
00:10:42,421 --> 00:10:44,120
from turning.

152
00:10:44,121 --> 00:10:47,220
Out here on the outside we have the dynamometer torque arm.

153
00:10:47,221 --> 00:10:51,080
The torque arm is nothing more than a load measuring device.

154
00:10:51,081 --> 00:10:56,840
This load cell down here at the bottom measures the force exerted, which resists the twisting

155
00:10:56,841 --> 00:11:00,860
of the dynamometer as it measures the power.

156
00:11:00,861 --> 00:11:04,360
Car manufacturers still test their own engines.

157
00:11:04,361 --> 00:11:10,160
But since 2005, SAE has required that horsepower tests be carried out under the watchful eye

158
00:11:10,161 --> 00:11:15,120
of an official witness if a manufacturer wants to earn the society's certification.

159
00:11:15,121 --> 00:11:17,720
General Motors does this on a voluntary basis.

160
00:11:17,721 --> 00:11:19,720
How are you doing?

161
00:11:19,721 --> 00:11:20,720
Pretty good.

162
00:11:20,721 --> 00:11:22,720
I'm the conscience of the manufacturer.

163
00:11:22,721 --> 00:11:24,720
I make sure that the test is run correctly

164
00:11:24,721 --> 00:11:27,720
according to the procedure, and that the certified numbers

165
00:11:27,721 --> 00:11:30,720
will be the numbers that will be reaching the public.

166
00:11:30,721 --> 00:11:34,720
With everything in place, this all-important test begins.

167
00:11:34,721 --> 00:11:37,720
Everything looks pretty good, and we're ready to start the test.

168
00:11:37,721 --> 00:11:38,720
Let's go.

169
00:11:43,720 --> 00:11:45,720
Only a few minutes into the test,

170
00:11:45,721 --> 00:11:50,720
Technicians detect a problem and stop the procedure to investigate.

171
00:11:50,721 --> 00:11:54,720
A slight rattle at certain speeds, which isn't normal.

172
00:11:54,721 --> 00:11:57,720
It could be a loose pin, something wrong with the prop.

173
00:11:57,721 --> 00:12:03,720
With the witness watching, the GM engineers need to fix the problem fast.

174
00:12:05,720 --> 00:12:09,720
After inspecting the prop shaft, we noticed that the guard was a little bit loose.

175
00:12:09,721 --> 00:12:12,720
We then made sure all the bolts were tightened on the prop shaft,

176
00:12:12,721 --> 00:12:15,440
shaft, make sure all the bolts are tight, and now we're ready to go.

177
00:12:16,720 --> 00:12:19,360
With the problem solved, the test continues.

178
00:12:21,840 --> 00:12:26,580
For the next five hours, the engine undergoes an exhaustive examination.

179
00:12:27,640 --> 00:12:29,960
Okay, that was the last test point. Everything looks good.

180
00:12:30,060 --> 00:12:32,040
Gotta go ahead and start the engine shutdown sequence.

181
00:12:33,800 --> 00:12:39,940
With the test completed, the question is, did the engine make the targeted 310 horsepower

182
00:12:39,941 --> 00:12:41,500
the manufacturer wants to claim?

183
00:12:42,720 --> 00:12:46,220
It's a tense time as the engineers and the witness

184
00:12:46,221 --> 00:12:47,760
pour over their calculations.

185
00:12:47,761 --> 00:12:49,860
It was a valid test.

186
00:12:49,861 --> 00:12:51,660
All the test parameters were within spec.

187
00:12:51,661 --> 00:12:52,960
We have good horsepower numbers,

188
00:12:52,961 --> 00:12:55,360
so it was a valid engine power test, and we can certify.

189
00:12:55,361 --> 00:12:58,640
Thank you very much.

190
00:12:58,641 --> 00:13:01,280
This is just one of the many engines General Motors

191
00:13:01,281 --> 00:13:03,200
has tested under SAE standards.

192
00:13:07,680 --> 00:13:11,180
They're three-ton boat beasts, careening across the water

193
00:13:11,181 --> 00:13:13,540
at speeds exceeding 200 miles per hour.

194
00:13:16,680 --> 00:13:20,680
Their powerful turbine engines generate up to 3,000 horsepower.

195
00:13:22,800 --> 00:13:25,220
This is the world of hydroplane racing.

196
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:38,520
Dave Villwock holds one of the world hydroplane speed records

197
00:13:38,521 --> 00:13:40,740
at 220 miles per hour.

198
00:13:41,180 --> 00:13:46,180
These are a 3,000-horsepower bucking Bronco.

199
00:13:46,181 --> 00:13:48,180
Everything about it is difficult to control,

200
00:13:48,181 --> 00:13:51,180
and a lot of times you're not in control.

201
00:13:54,180 --> 00:13:56,180
We just try to do the best we can.

202
00:13:56,181 --> 00:14:01,180
Since the 1980s, most hydroplane races have been won by boats

203
00:14:01,181 --> 00:14:05,180
using a turbine engine, originally designed for Vietnam-era

204
00:14:05,181 --> 00:14:07,180
Chinook helicopters.

205
00:14:07,181 --> 00:14:14,180
These craft half skim, half fly across the surface of the water.

206
00:14:14,181 --> 00:14:19,180
Only the propeller is underwater as they throttle to full speed.

207
00:14:19,181 --> 00:14:22,180
The hydroplane is part airplane, part boat.

208
00:14:22,181 --> 00:14:27,180
On my right foot, we have a throttle pedal that controls the engine throttle.

209
00:14:27,181 --> 00:14:35,180
On the left side, we have a flight control pedal, which controls a forward flap that either makes the boat go up or down.

210
00:14:35,181 --> 00:14:36,340
the boat go up or down.

211
00:14:40,620 --> 00:14:43,260
So we're flying the boats like you would an airplane,

212
00:14:43,320 --> 00:14:46,520
just very, very close to the water, hopefully.

213
00:14:46,600 --> 00:14:50,300
Hydroplane racing is a summer sport.

214
00:14:50,360 --> 00:14:52,260
During the off-season, the boats and engines

215
00:14:52,340 --> 00:14:56,160
go through a complete overhaul.

216
00:14:56,240 --> 00:15:00,400
At the turbine engine warehouse near Seattle, Washington,

217
00:15:00,480 --> 00:15:04,200
this engine is fired up to assess that all 3,000 horsepower

218
00:15:04,201 --> 00:15:05,700
will function for the next season.

219
00:15:12,900 --> 00:15:15,020
They don't call them thunderboats for nothing.

220
00:15:16,040 --> 00:15:17,980
The extreme noise is deafening.

221
00:15:19,420 --> 00:15:21,420
Although it may sound like a jet engine,

222
00:15:21,880 --> 00:15:25,720
it doesn't provide thrust, but rather rotation.

223
00:15:26,720 --> 00:15:28,820
We have rotors that are spinning

224
00:15:28,821 --> 00:15:31,620
anywhere from 18,000 revolutions per minute

225
00:15:31,621 --> 00:15:35,380
to 15,000 revolution per minute for the power turbines.

226
00:15:41,180 --> 00:15:44,300
The turbine engine makes horsepower in four stages.

227
00:15:44,740 --> 00:15:49,300
Intake, compression, combustion, and exhaust.

228
00:15:49,800 --> 00:15:51,580
That makes a very efficient engine.

229
00:15:52,700 --> 00:15:58,160
In stage one, the turbine engine takes in air at 300 cubic feet per second.

230
00:15:58,161 --> 00:16:06,000
Stage two, the air goes through a compressor, which pressurizes it to 90 pounds per square

231
00:16:06,001 --> 00:16:08,440
inch.

232
00:16:08,441 --> 00:16:15,120
Stage three, the air is combined with fuel in the burner can and is ignited.

233
00:16:15,121 --> 00:16:22,380
And finally, stage four, the supercharged hot gases spin the main turbine assembly.

234
00:16:22,381 --> 00:16:24,260
This is the power turbine assembly.

235
00:16:24,261 --> 00:16:26,440
This is where the horsepower is made.

236
00:16:26,441 --> 00:16:30,400
gases that are about 1500 degrees Fahrenheit push on all these little

237
00:16:30,401 --> 00:16:34,600
blades there's lots of gas lots of little blades this wheel is just like a

238
00:16:34,601 --> 00:16:39,260
pinwheel all these little blades are at an angle it spins this power turbine

239
00:16:39,261 --> 00:16:45,280
assembly 15,000 330 revolutions per minute it turns the shaft the same speed

240
00:16:45,281 --> 00:16:49,840
the shaft is attached to the propeller which pushes the race boat forward and

241
00:16:49,841 --> 00:16:54,220
And that's what gives the hydroplane its tremendous speed and horsepower.

242
00:16:55,920 --> 00:17:00,900
With all this explosive horsepower, you might think the engine needs highly combustible fuel.

243
00:17:01,880 --> 00:17:05,900
Everybody thinks that we use exotic fuels in these engines, but we don't.

244
00:17:06,360 --> 00:17:10,900
What we have is basically home heating oil, which is essentially kerosene.

245
00:17:13,440 --> 00:17:17,260
We use kerosene because it's a very stable fuel.

246
00:17:17,261 --> 00:17:20,000
The risk of fire in the boat is very low.

247
00:17:20,160 --> 00:17:25,240
And also, we can extract a lot of energy out of every gallon of fuel versus a lot of other fuels.

248
00:17:26,880 --> 00:17:29,940
It's one thing to harness 3,000 horsepower on land,

249
00:17:30,420 --> 00:17:33,540
but it's quite another on the ever-changing surface of water.

250
00:17:41,120 --> 00:17:43,080
Drivers are put to the ultimate test.

251
00:17:43,780 --> 00:17:46,100
You're essentially an accident waiting to happen.

252
00:17:46,101 --> 00:17:49,440
when you're going so fast, you cover a football field

253
00:17:49,441 --> 00:17:50,400
in a second.

254
00:17:50,401 --> 00:17:51,940
So when you're looking for something to happen,

255
00:17:51,941 --> 00:17:54,980
you're looking way down the track.

256
00:17:54,981 --> 00:17:57,320
Ken Muscatel is a hydroplane driver

257
00:17:57,321 --> 00:18:00,340
who lost his battle with horsepower in a spectacular

258
00:18:00,341 --> 00:18:01,480
crash.

259
00:18:06,360 --> 00:18:10,220
The boat was going good, about 185, not super fast.

260
00:18:10,221 --> 00:18:13,000
And all of a sudden, it just starts lifting.

261
00:18:13,001 --> 00:18:14,060
And it's not coming down.

262
00:18:14,061 --> 00:18:16,320
and it goes higher and higher and higher,

263
00:18:16,321 --> 00:18:18,640
probably ended up 60 feet in the air,

264
00:18:18,641 --> 00:18:20,800
flew all the way around 360,

265
00:18:20,801 --> 00:18:23,540
landed on his tail, slapped down back on the water,

266
00:18:23,541 --> 00:18:26,380
broke the boat in half, but was kept going,

267
00:18:26,381 --> 00:18:27,300
so I had to shut it off,

268
00:18:27,301 --> 00:18:29,080
and I just waited for people to come.

269
00:18:31,720 --> 00:18:34,280
I felt lucky, lucky to be alive.

270
00:18:34,281 --> 00:18:36,080
Oh, my God!

271
00:18:38,120 --> 00:18:40,480
Lucky not to be hurt, and still disappointed

272
00:18:40,481 --> 00:18:42,860
because we weren't gonna be able to race the boat.

273
00:18:44,060 --> 00:18:51,520
With all the speed and danger, have hydroplanes reached the outer limits of horsepower?

274
00:18:54,020 --> 00:19:01,300
Bottom line is that in the future of hydroplane racing, I see more technology, more power,

275
00:19:02,760 --> 00:19:03,920
faster race boats.

276
00:19:05,280 --> 00:19:09,160
But high horsepower on water doesn't always mean high speed.

277
00:19:10,340 --> 00:19:12,740
It can also determine sheer muscle.

278
00:19:15,060 --> 00:19:20,400
A motorboat engine operating at around 90 horsepower can haul your average water skier.

279
00:19:20,860 --> 00:19:23,920
But for the heaviest loads on Earth, you need a heavy-duty engine.

280
00:19:25,780 --> 00:19:29,400
One of the most powerful diesel engines in the world when it first launched,

281
00:19:29,960 --> 00:19:35,120
the Wartzilla RTA 96C has an astounding 95,000 horsepower.

282
00:19:36,380 --> 00:19:39,260
This behemoth was designed to power mammoth ships,

283
00:19:39,261 --> 00:19:42,700
like this 72,000-ton cargo container ship

284
00:19:42,701 --> 00:19:45,160
originally built by the Costco shipping company.

285
00:19:46,100 --> 00:19:47,120
Manufactured in Korea,

286
00:19:47,640 --> 00:19:51,660
the Watsilla engine is 76 feet long and 44 feet high,

287
00:19:52,080 --> 00:19:54,100
as large as a three-story house.

288
00:19:56,620 --> 00:19:59,060
I've been working on big engines all my life.

289
00:19:59,500 --> 00:20:00,360
This is the biggest.

290
00:20:00,840 --> 00:20:01,540
It's amazing.

291
00:20:01,780 --> 00:20:02,980
It's a challenge.

292
00:20:03,840 --> 00:20:05,940
It's a lot of horsepower to look after.

293
00:20:06,460 --> 00:20:07,640
It's a big engine, massive.

294
00:20:09,260 --> 00:20:12,660
The total engine weight is 2,300 tons.

295
00:20:14,940 --> 00:20:17,860
The crankshaft alone weighs 300 tons.

296
00:20:18,780 --> 00:20:22,140
It's over 1,000 times more powerful than many car engines.

297
00:20:24,880 --> 00:20:29,100
Each of the Wartzilla's 12 cylinders delivers 8,000 horsepower.

298
00:20:30,960 --> 00:20:33,360
This is a spare piston for this main engine.

299
00:20:34,240 --> 00:20:37,740
It's 20 feet tall, and it weighs 5 and 1⁄2 tons.

300
00:20:37,741 --> 00:20:43,480
Just imagine this piston moving up and down the engine at 102 times per minute.

301
00:20:43,880 --> 00:20:44,720
That's incredible.

302
00:20:46,620 --> 00:20:50,520
Inside the 12 assembly units where the pistons are connected to the crankshaft,

303
00:20:51,960 --> 00:20:53,680
technicians inspect the engine parts.

304
00:20:55,380 --> 00:20:59,580
Running at full power, the Wartzilla would be far too dangerous to approach this close.

305
00:21:01,240 --> 00:21:04,100
So it slowed down to a fraction of its normal speed.

306
00:21:07,740 --> 00:21:10,040
We are slowly turning the engine at the moment,

307
00:21:10,280 --> 00:21:14,460
just to give you an idea of this massive piece of steel coming up,

308
00:21:14,720 --> 00:21:16,300
driven from the pistons.

309
00:21:17,300 --> 00:21:19,300
When this machine is in operation,

310
00:21:19,520 --> 00:21:21,660
you will never be able to stand in this area.

311
00:21:22,880 --> 00:21:25,120
However, using time-lapse photography,

312
00:21:25,580 --> 00:21:28,500
we're able to speed up the process and bring the engine to life.

313
00:21:30,980 --> 00:21:33,380
With each stroke of the 5.5-ton assembly,

314
00:21:33,381 --> 00:21:35,460
it covers more than 8 feet.

315
00:21:37,740 --> 00:21:40,980
This enormous mass of metal drives the crankshaft,

316
00:21:40,981 --> 00:21:43,740
which generates the 95,000 horsepower.

317
00:21:47,680 --> 00:21:51,320
The engine consumes up to 250 tons of fuel a day.

318
00:21:53,800 --> 00:21:57,800
It can propel this massive vessel at up to 24 knots,

319
00:21:59,220 --> 00:22:02,420
a blazing speed for a container ship this size.

320
00:22:02,421 --> 00:22:09,300
It gives you a feeling like a car driver.

321
00:22:09,800 --> 00:22:14,320
If he has a fast car on a good road without speed limit,

322
00:22:14,540 --> 00:22:16,720
where you can use his horsepowers.

323
00:22:17,380 --> 00:22:18,760
So it is a nice feeling.

324
00:22:20,120 --> 00:22:22,360
Because of the rising demand for global shipping,

325
00:22:23,020 --> 00:22:24,900
bigger and faster ships are sure to follow.

326
00:22:27,240 --> 00:22:29,940
Propulsion isn't the only use for tremendous horsepower.

327
00:22:29,941 --> 00:22:33,080
it's also harnessed to generate electric power.

328
00:22:33,880 --> 00:22:34,920
When the lights go out,

329
00:22:35,480 --> 00:22:38,140
people's lives depend on these monstrous engines.

330
00:22:41,960 --> 00:22:44,020
Welcome to Waukesha, Wisconsin,

331
00:22:44,660 --> 00:22:48,260
a tranquil, unassuming town in middle America.

332
00:22:50,420 --> 00:22:53,320
However, it's also home to incredible horsepower.

333
00:22:57,560 --> 00:22:59,460
Here at Waukesha Engine.

334
00:22:59,940 --> 00:23:03,940
Many people have maybe never heard the name of Waukesha Engine,

335
00:23:03,941 --> 00:23:06,940
but it's out there producing power throughout the world.

336
00:23:06,941 --> 00:23:11,940
Today, Waukesha Engine uses its Wisconsin facility for research and development.

337
00:23:11,941 --> 00:23:20,940
But not long ago, this one million square foot factory had more than 800 workers design, build and ship mammoth engine generators

338
00:23:20,941 --> 00:23:23,940
that deliver essential electric power around the globe.

339
00:23:23,941 --> 00:23:26,440
the globe.

340
00:23:26,441 --> 00:23:29,280
Waukesha engines are painted bright orange and roar

341
00:23:29,281 --> 00:23:30,580
while operating.

342
00:23:30,581 --> 00:23:32,420
These features have earned them the nickname

343
00:23:32,421 --> 00:23:33,420
Thunder Pumpkins.

344
00:23:36,120 --> 00:23:40,180
Among these is the Advanced Power Generation System,

345
00:23:40,181 --> 00:23:44,800
or APG 3000, which produces 4,400 horsepower.

346
00:23:50,460 --> 00:23:53,940
Waukesha engines are about horsepower, pure and simple.

347
00:23:53,941 --> 00:23:57,700
The way that we get that horsepower is we throw a lot of iron at it.

348
00:23:57,701 --> 00:24:02,940
The pistons are bigger, the block is bigger, the connecting rods, everything's just bigger,

349
00:24:02,941 --> 00:24:09,120
beefier to handle the durability and reliability that the customers demand.

350
00:24:09,121 --> 00:24:13,780
In a blackout, a portable 7-horsepower generator can help get the lights back on.

351
00:24:13,781 --> 00:24:18,980
But a bigger outage requires a vastly bigger engine.

352
00:24:18,981 --> 00:24:22,860
This APG engine is 11 feet high and 25 feet long

353
00:24:22,861 --> 00:24:24,980
and weighs in at a massive 40 tons.

354
00:24:28,980 --> 00:24:33,500
It's a big engine with an even bigger job to perform.

355
00:24:33,501 --> 00:24:35,300
The customers that buy these engines

356
00:24:35,301 --> 00:24:37,220
are in very critical applications.

357
00:24:37,221 --> 00:24:40,780
In a lot of places, we are their only source of electricity,

358
00:24:40,781 --> 00:24:44,500
be it in foreign countries where there is no electrical grid,

359
00:24:44,501 --> 00:24:47,380
be it in hospitals where there's a power outage.

360
00:24:47,381 --> 00:24:51,380
They cannot go down because people's lives often depend

361
00:24:51,381 --> 00:24:55,580
on these engines operating reliably for as long as they can.

362
00:24:55,581 --> 00:24:58,560
These engines use an internal combustion system

363
00:24:58,561 --> 00:25:01,780
that employs pistons connected to a crankshaft,

364
00:25:01,781 --> 00:25:02,780
much like in a car.

365
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:10,440
Besides the size, the biggest difference is the fuel.

366
00:25:10,441 --> 00:25:16,220
Instead of gasoline, the APG runs on natural gas.

367
00:25:16,221 --> 00:25:21,640
Natural gas is a very clean burning fuel and very friendly to the environment, much more so than gasoline.

368
00:25:22,100 --> 00:25:26,620
It's very plentiful around the world in remote locations, so it's easily available.

369
00:25:27,120 --> 00:25:35,160
The APG can draw natural gas straight from ground deposits or even draw methane gas from a garbage landfill.

370
00:25:35,820 --> 00:25:40,100
It always has a steady supply of power and never has to be refueled.

371
00:25:40,101 --> 00:25:45,900
This combination of engine and generator is known as an

372
00:25:45,901 --> 00:25:46,900
Ningenator.

373
00:25:46,901 --> 00:25:51,420
The Ningenators are designed to last for years and years.

374
00:25:51,421 --> 00:25:54,280
Most of the major components last for the lifetime of the

375
00:25:54,281 --> 00:25:55,280
engine.

376
00:25:55,281 --> 00:26:00,960
Construction of the APG begins when raw stock from an iron

377
00:26:00,961 --> 00:26:02,120
foundry is delivered.

378
00:26:02,121 --> 00:26:09,220
It'll take up to four weeks before the 8,700-pound crankcase

379
00:26:09,221 --> 00:26:16,460
and 3,700-pound crankshaft reach what is known as engine tolerance.

380
00:26:16,560 --> 00:26:19,060
Tolerances are how close these parts fit together.

381
00:26:19,160 --> 00:26:21,460
They have to seal a lot of pressure from combustion,

382
00:26:21,560 --> 00:26:23,260
and there's a lot of moving parts.

383
00:26:23,360 --> 00:26:25,640
And because you're going through so many cycles,

384
00:26:25,740 --> 00:26:28,100
it's important that they fit to be extremely close.

385
00:26:30,940 --> 00:26:36,480
The crankshaft is holed onto a computerized lathe,

386
00:26:36,481 --> 00:26:39,360
where almost a quarter of its size will be shaved off.

387
00:26:41,360 --> 00:26:44,100
We'll be removing 600 pounds from this crankshaft

388
00:26:44,101 --> 00:26:45,640
as it's processed down the line.

389
00:26:46,000 --> 00:26:49,160
We'll be finishing those diameters down to a width of a human hair.

390
00:26:49,520 --> 00:26:53,640
We need this exacting tolerance in order for the engine to operate properly in the field.

391
00:26:55,120 --> 00:26:57,580
The main part of the engine, the crankcase,

392
00:26:57,920 --> 00:26:59,980
undergoes a process known as machining,

393
00:27:01,580 --> 00:27:05,700
in which computer-assisted cutting tools drill cylinders for the pistons

394
00:27:05,701 --> 00:27:07,200
that will eventually be installed.

395
00:27:08,960 --> 00:27:12,040
We machine off over 1,500 pounds

396
00:27:12,041 --> 00:27:14,940
to get that piece of iron into the dimensions

397
00:27:14,941 --> 00:27:17,080
that we require for quality.

398
00:27:18,820 --> 00:27:22,160
The 42-pound pistons also go through the machining process

399
00:27:22,161 --> 00:27:25,700
until they shine like jewels.

400
00:27:29,700 --> 00:27:32,340
These are aluminum pistons, and when you machine them,

401
00:27:32,341 --> 00:27:34,960
they get the shiny appearance, but don't let that fool you.

402
00:27:34,961 --> 00:27:36,800
When these are in operation in the engine,

403
00:27:36,801 --> 00:27:38,460
it's a very harsh environment.

404
00:27:38,461 --> 00:27:40,760
When you have this combustion, this huge explosion

405
00:27:40,761 --> 00:27:42,760
that goes on in the cylinder, this piston

406
00:27:42,761 --> 00:27:45,700
has to withstand that pressure, which can be as high as 2,000

407
00:27:45,701 --> 00:27:47,200
pounds per square inch.

408
00:27:47,201 --> 00:27:50,080
And that's what creates your horsepower that's transmitted

409
00:27:50,081 --> 00:27:51,720
out the crankshaft.

410
00:27:51,721 --> 00:27:54,320
When all the main engine parts are ready for assembly,

411
00:27:54,321 --> 00:27:57,620
they're placed on a rollover.

412
00:27:57,621 --> 00:28:01,460
It's something like an enormous engine rotisserie.

413
00:28:01,461 --> 00:28:03,880
On the rollover that you see on the assembly line,

414
00:28:03,881 --> 00:28:06,420
where the crankcase is first mated with the crankshaft

415
00:28:06,421 --> 00:28:08,080
and the other major components,

416
00:28:08,081 --> 00:28:10,180
you're really looking at the birth of an engine.

417
00:28:10,181 --> 00:28:11,720
That's where it all comes together,

418
00:28:11,721 --> 00:28:13,420
and that's where it starts its life.

419
00:28:18,820 --> 00:28:23,140
The last stage for the APG engine is a break-in test.

420
00:28:23,141 --> 00:28:24,540
It's a five-hour test.

421
00:28:24,541 --> 00:28:27,300
We gradually increase the speeds and loads.

422
00:28:27,301 --> 00:28:29,400
This allows us to break in the components.

423
00:28:29,401 --> 00:28:31,440
Remember, there's many tight tolerances

424
00:28:31,441 --> 00:28:33,240
that need to be carefully broken in

425
00:28:33,241 --> 00:28:36,280
so the product will last a long time.

426
00:28:36,380 --> 00:28:38,120
And maybe there's still great horsepower

427
00:28:38,220 --> 00:28:42,620
to come from these Waukesha giants.

428
00:28:42,720 --> 00:28:45,220
Horsepower will get higher in steps,

429
00:28:45,320 --> 00:28:46,960
but in order to get there, the engineers

430
00:28:47,060 --> 00:28:51,160
will have to look at things such as new metals, new controls,

431
00:28:51,260 --> 00:28:55,060
new emissions technologies to get that horsepower up to, say,

432
00:28:55,160 --> 00:28:58,740
15% to 20% higher out of the same weight of iron.

433
00:29:03,240 --> 00:29:06,960
This is the Falcon Heavy rocket ship by SpaceX.

434
00:29:07,620 --> 00:29:11,540
Its engines generate over 5 million pounds of thrust at takeoff,

435
00:29:12,300 --> 00:29:15,420
capable of lifting 18 747 aircrafts.

436
00:29:20,880 --> 00:29:25,020
But that pales in comparison to the most powerful engine ever built,

437
00:29:25,680 --> 00:29:27,020
the Saturn V rocket.

438
00:29:30,280 --> 00:29:32,940
This behemoth flew a generation ago.

439
00:29:33,240 --> 00:29:35,680
and sent American astronauts to the moon.

440
00:29:37,140 --> 00:29:40,720
The Saturn V produced 7.5 million pounds of thrust,

441
00:29:41,260 --> 00:29:45,400
which converts to an unbelievable 160 million horsepower.

442
00:29:48,680 --> 00:29:52,200
This enormous horsepower was used to break the bonds of gravity.

443
00:29:54,140 --> 00:29:56,340
But if we turn the equation around,

444
00:29:57,660 --> 00:30:01,560
gravity itself can be used to unleash an astronomical amount of horsepower.

445
00:30:03,240 --> 00:30:13,440
At Hoover Dam on the Nevada, Arizona border, falling water has been harnessed to produce

446
00:30:13,441 --> 00:30:16,560
more than 2 million horsepower.

447
00:30:16,561 --> 00:30:27,840
Water is heavy, and when gravity tries to pull water down, I think about 3,200 cubic

448
00:30:27,841 --> 00:30:32,940
feet per second dropping from the top of Hoover Dam

449
00:30:32,941 --> 00:30:34,920
to the base of the dam.

450
00:30:34,921 --> 00:30:36,720
And most people can see that that's

451
00:30:36,721 --> 00:30:38,820
kind of like the energy of a freight train.

452
00:30:42,760 --> 00:30:48,820
Completed in 1936, Hoover Dam stands at 726 feet

453
00:30:48,821 --> 00:30:50,620
and is still the tallest concrete dam

454
00:30:50,621 --> 00:30:51,660
in the Western Hemisphere.

455
00:30:51,661 --> 00:30:58,060
It's 45 feet thick at the top.

456
00:30:58,061 --> 00:30:59,660
It gets a little bit thicker at the base.

457
00:30:59,661 --> 00:31:02,260
It is 660 feet thick at the base,

458
00:31:02,261 --> 00:31:04,460
thicker than two football fields long.

459
00:31:04,461 --> 00:31:08,660
3,250,000 cubic yards of concrete in the dam,

460
00:31:08,661 --> 00:31:11,360
and it weighs in at 6.6 million tons,

461
00:31:11,361 --> 00:31:14,180
enough concrete to make a two-lane highway from San

462
00:31:14,181 --> 00:31:16,820
Francisco to New York City.

463
00:31:16,821 --> 00:31:19,700
By holding back the mighty Colorado River,

464
00:31:19,701 --> 00:31:23,800
the dam generated Lake Mead, which is close to 500 feet deep

465
00:31:23,801 --> 00:31:26,200
and holds over 9 trillion gallons of water.

466
00:31:28,940 --> 00:31:32,460
The big picture is the water starts out in Lake Mead,

467
00:31:32,461 --> 00:31:36,780
high up on this structure, right near the top.

468
00:31:36,781 --> 00:31:41,100
The deeper down in that water we go, the higher the pressure.

469
00:31:41,101 --> 00:31:43,280
We deliver the water to the turbines

470
00:31:43,281 --> 00:31:46,240
at a pressure of 250 pounds per square inch.

471
00:31:49,700 --> 00:31:53,700
At the bottom of Hoover Dam are 17 reaction turbines.

472
00:31:56,700 --> 00:32:01,700
Each turbine produces between 150 and 180,000 horsepower.

473
00:32:05,700 --> 00:32:09,700
The way a turbine works is like one of the old fashioned sprinklers

474
00:32:09,701 --> 00:32:11,700
that brings the water up the center column,

475
00:32:11,701 --> 00:32:14,700
and then across the top of the sprinkler,

476
00:32:14,701 --> 00:32:16,700
when the water shoots out of the angled nozzles,

477
00:32:16,701 --> 00:32:23,980
nozzles the sprinkler spins the other way and that's identical to a reaction turbine a massive

478
00:32:23,981 --> 00:32:29,660
steel and copper shaft delivers the horsepower from the turbine spinning below to the generator

479
00:32:30,220 --> 00:32:38,380
which produces the electricity i'm standing on top of a generator this generator raised 1.2

480
00:32:38,381 --> 00:32:47,980
million pounds and you can see it spinning that shaft is spinning 180 rpm or three times per

481
00:32:47,981 --> 00:32:56,220
second so as you watch that shaft spin you're watching the top end of 1.2 million pounds of

482
00:32:56,221 --> 00:33:04,060
rotating copper and steel the purpose of this generator that we're standing on is to convert

483
00:33:04,061 --> 00:33:07,840
that horsepower in the water that's flowing into the turbine

484
00:33:07,841 --> 00:33:12,400
into electricity so that we can send that electricity out

485
00:33:12,401 --> 00:33:17,000
on the wires to our customers in the cities.

486
00:33:17,001 --> 00:33:18,940
The horsepower that drives the generators

487
00:33:18,941 --> 00:33:22,780
can produce more than 2,000 megawatts of electrical power

